{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>I returned.</b> Solomon said 'I returned and reconsidered,' that I had praised joy, and that the good that a person should seek is pleasure, because no person can rejoice in this wealth, nor take pleasure in it, for I saw that the swift stumble, and those who have no strength to run flee and escape." "<b>And not to the mighty is battle.</b> As in the matter of 'and the mighty man does not escape by great strength.'" "<b>And also not to the wise is bread.</b> For it would be fitting for them to rule over fools, and they would be wealthy, but the matter is mostly the opposite." "<b>And also not to those who know favor.</b> In the matter of governance, that they would love him and turn to his authority." "<b>For time and chance befall them all.</b> The matter of 'them all' — the wise and the fools and the mighty and the weak. And what is correct in my eyes is that 'them all' refers only to those mentioned. And the meaning is that no capability is in the hand of man to do this. And the proof: the wise, masters of ideas, fathers of crafts — they have no bread. And those who say 'whatever your hand finds to do, do with all your strength' — it is folly, and a thing that will not stand. And the meaning of עת is what is in the arrangement of the uppermost celestial system. And the meaning of פגע is the encounter of one of the planetary spheres with one of the seven: which is its half, and its half before it and after it, and its third, and its half before it and after it. And know that the word עת is missing a nun. And now I will explain. Know that in the holy language there is no letter that transforms and interchanges and is assimilated and added and missing except the letters alef, he, vav, yod, as R. Judah son of R. David the grammarian, of blessed memory, explained. And the letter nun is found assimilated and missing at the beginning, as in נסע where the nun is assimilated by a dagesh — ויסע, and likewise וישא, ויתן, ויטע, וידר, ויפול, ויגוף, ויבול עלהו. And in the pu'al the nuns all appear: נוסע, נושא, נותן, נוטע, נודר, נופל, נוגף, נובל. And in the imperative the nuns drop, as in קומו סעו שאו את ראש, תנו יד לה׳. And sometimes the nuns remain in their pattern, as in נפלו עלינו. And in the middle it is found assimilated with a second nun in the word שתי, for it is from ולא אשנה לו. And likewise בת, missing the nun of בן. And so עת is ענת, and like it in the words of our Sages of blessed memory: עונה. And so in Aramaic: וכענת. And the dagesh of the tav of עת is for the assimilation of the nun, like the dagesh of the tav in התיו and the nun of אני in the word אתה. And likewise חטים, and both appear in Aramaic. And the tav of אמתו, for its root is from אמונה, and the tav is feminine and indicates it, as in ותהי האמת נעדרת. And likewise the tav of עת is feminine and indicates it. And according to this grammatical analysis, לעות את יעף remains alone, and it has no companion in Scripture."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:54.157204644Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}